Q: How to make a parallelogram in React Native A: <pre><code>This is a known bug that's been around for a hot min! lol 2020ish. There's a GitHub issue for it (#27649) that was technically closed with a fix, but clearly it's still broken for a lot of people depending on the RN version or what else you're combining the transform with.

  Since you can't really count on the native skew transform working consistently on Android, your best bet is to just draw the shape yourself. If you need a gradient fill anyway, react-native-svg makes this pretty painless:

  import Svg, { Polygon, Defs, LinearGradient, Stop } from 'react-native-svg';

  const Parallelogram = ({ width, height, skew }) =&gt; (
    &lt;Svg width={width + skew} height={height}&gt;
      &lt;Defs&gt;
        &lt;LinearGradient id=&quot;grad&quot; x1=&quot;0&quot; y1=&quot;0&quot; x2=&quot;1&quot; y2=&quot;0&quot;&gt;
          &lt;Stop offset=&quot;0&quot; stopColor=&quot;#ff00ff&quot; /&gt;
          &lt;Stop offset=&quot;1&quot; stopColor=&quot;#0066ff&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/LinearGradient&gt;
      &lt;/Defs&gt;
      &lt;Polygon
        points={`${skew},0 ${width + skew},0 ${width},${height} 0,${height}`}
        fill=&quot;url(#grad)&quot;
      /&gt;
    &lt;/Svg&gt;
  );

  Or if you're using react-native-skia:

  import { Canvas, Path, LinearGradient, vec } from '@shopify/react-native-skia';

  const Parallelogram = ({ width, height, skew }) =&gt; (
    &lt;Canvas style={{ width: width + skew, height }}&gt;
      &lt;Path
        path={`M ${skew} 0 L ${width + skew} 0 L ${width} ${height} L 0 ${height} Z`}
      &gt;
        &lt;LinearGradient
          start={vec(0, 0)}
          end={vec(width, 0)}
          colors={['#ff00ff', '#0066ff']}
        /&gt;
      &lt;/Path&gt;
    &lt;/Canvas&gt;
  );

  Both work consistently across iOS and Android since you're not relying on the busted transform system at all. Hope This Helps!!!
</code></pre>

Q: How to compare two structs in golang but ignore undefined fields A: <p>you can not reload operator in Go like c++</p>
<p>but you shall def your own method for struct like this</p>
<pre><code>package main

import &quot;fmt&quot;

type MyType struct {
    foo string
    bar string
}

// Equal provides a custom equality check for MyType.
func (m MyType) Equal(other MyType) bool {
    return m.foo == other.foo
}

func main() {
    a := MyType{foo: &quot;foo&quot;}
    b := MyType{foo: &quot;foo&quot;, bar: &quot;bar&quot;}
    c := MyType{foo: &quot;foo&quot;, bar: &quot;baz&quot;}
    if a.Equal(b) {
        fmt.Println(&quot;a is equal to b&quot;)
    }
    if b.Equal(c) {
        fmt.Println(&quot;b is equal to c&quot;)
    } else {
        fmt.Println(&quot;b is not equal to c&quot;)
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: What does `[…](…) { … }` mean inside an object definition in JavaScript? A: <p>You would use the square brackets when u want to dynamically compute an object literal's property name. For example</p>

<pre><code>function foo() {
    // some logic

    return 'foo';

}

 // some variable in your code
 const bar = 'bar'

 // using square brackets to dynamically compute property names for the object
 const obj = {
   [foo()]: 'hello',
   [bar]: 'there'
 }

 // console.log(obj.foo) will show 'hello'
 // console.log(obj.bar) will show 'there'
</code></pre>

<p>This is very useful when you want an object's property name to be dynamic. However
the <code>[Symbol.asyncIterator]()</code> property is somewhat special. For an object to be an async iterable(which means you can use <code>for await ...of</code> to iterate over the object) it has to follow certain protocols in javascript. The property <code>[Symbol.asyncIterator]</code> as a function defines the default async iterator for an object. You can read more about it here.
<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Symbol/asyncIterator" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Symbol/asyncIterator</a></p>
